If you attempted to answer this question, you will realize that it is
simple question but difficult to answer. So, as you stop reading this
book for a moment, take a break to really answer this question; ‘Who
am I?’

You probably answered this question by stating your profession,
title, office, position, nationality, qualifications, certifications, family
pedigree or where you work, school or live. Probably you said, ‘I am
a professor, doctor, pastor, engineer, guru, CEO, teacher, business
man, president . . .’ If you answered with any of the fore-listed, you
have just attached your essence and entirety to an external parameter
which may change. If you defined who you are by any of this above
you are in serious danger.

If you answered, ‘I am a doctor’, then a good question to ask you is,
‘who were you before you became a doctor’? A nobody? What if you
lost your job or ability to be a doctor today, who then do you
become? A nobody? If for whatever reason, you lose the position or
status you occupy today, who then are you?

                                                            16
                                 THE MYSTERY OF SELF PERCEPTION

Do you now understand why people commit suicide when they lose
their wealth, job, position etc? It is because of identity-crisis. They
cannot imagine life without the position, status or possession that
they have used as the definition of their entire essence.
‘Who are you? ‘is a question of self-definition, self-perception, self-
esteem and life-assignment.
