Identifying your Life Assignment

Some major principles of ‘The Life Assignment’ is that your assignment
must reveal the heart of God to people, solve problems for people and
advance God’s agenda on earth. One mistake people make is that when
they think life assignment, they usually think in terms of career, job or
activity. They think “Life Assignment” and they think, ‘Am I to be a
doctor, banker or lawyer’. Many times, Christians who sense a call into
the ministry equally make the mistake of considering their life
assignments in terms of ministry offices. They are quick to ask, ‘Am I
called to be an apostle, prophet, evangelist, pastor or teacher’. Life
Assignment is not a career path or a ministry office. It is a job
description.

The truth is that thinking in terms of career title or ministry appellation
will not give you the right premise for discovering your life assignment.
A better understanding of life assignment makes you think in term of
assignment and task rather than titles, careers or offices. Thus, in
discovering your life assignments. there are questions to reflect on. One
distinguishing ability of successful people is the ability to ask the right
questions.

                                                            36
                                           THE LIFE ASSIGNMENT

Getting the right answer starts with asking the right questions. Some
right questions to ask yourself regarding your life assignment include:

  1.What contribution do I believe I was created to make to my
     world and generation?’

  2.What legacy do I want to be remembered for when I leave the
     earth?’

  3.Whose lives will be testimonials that I lived?’
  4.What will be my contribution to God’s Agenda on earth before I

     transit to eternity?’
  5.Who and how many people will be impacted because I lived?’
  6.What sector of life or society is my life to affect?
  7.What gap am I to fill in life and for what category of people?
  8.What problem am I to solve and in what area of human life?
  9.What task am I on earth to do for God?’
The answers to these questions may not come easily, therefore, do
not feel under pressure to answer them quickly. However, as you
keep pondering, reflecting and most importantly, praying, about
these questions in daily life and walk with God, you will eventually
be led to fulfill your assignment.

                                                            37
                                           THE LIFE ASSIGNMENT

Themes of Life Assignment

There are three central themes around a viable life assignment. They
include God, a task, and a people. Thinking around these three themes
bring clarity to your life assignment. Your life assignment is all about
the task God sent you towards a particular people.

This understanding underscores the difference between career and
assignment. While a career or ministry could be a vital platform for
carrying out your assignment, it is not the assignment. Thus, becoming
a doctor is not a life assignment or purpose, but believing you are sent
by God to bring healing to the hurting through medicine is a life
assignment. Being a political leader is not a life assignment, but
knowing that you were created by God to bring integrity to the
governance of a people, community or nation is a life assignment.
Being a pastor or apostle is not a life assignment, but knowing that
God has called you to get people saved and establish a local assembly
where they will be nurtured to grow into all that God wants them to be
is a life assignment. One could be a doctor and not fulfil his/her life
assignment. One could be an apostle and be missing his/her life
assignment. Your life assignment is to be thought about in terms of a
task, message, and job description. Not a career path or a ministry
appellation.

                                                            38
                                             THE LIFE ASSIGNMENT

Other important elements of a life assignment are the location,
duration, specificity and environment of the assignment. However,
these all may change with time. The environment refers to the sphere of
the assignment. The environment could be secular or spiritual,
mundane or religious, business or informal.

